LESSONS_P2 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The item's buying side: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module covers purchase items, units, costs, and planning fields in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the item's buying field set on the one register record; operate purchase units of measure with their conversions; maintain buying price lists and supplier rates; distinguish the three figures called cost and use each correctly; complete the planning fields that drive replenishment; read the last purchase rate and the price trend; and compute in landed-cost terms for imported goods.</p><p><b>One record, both sides of trade.</b> The Item is one record serving selling and buying. The Selling track completed the commercial section; this module completes the buying section — how the item is purchased, in what units, from which suppliers, at what agreed rates, with what planning parameters. The one-record principle is restated because the most common structural error on the buying side is its violation: <b>a separate buying item is never created for a product the register already holds.</b> A PARA-500-BUY record beside PARA-500 splits the item's stock, cost history, and planning arithmetic into two half-records, each wrong; the buying section of the existing record is completed instead, through the item gate the Inventory track defined.</p><p><b>What buying reads from the Item.</b></p><p>1. <b>Is Purchase Item</b> — the flag that admits the item to purchasing documents: the buying mirror of the selling flag, and the first check when an item will not appear on a Purchase Order.<br>2. <b>The purchase units</b> — the default purchase unit and the conversion table (Chapter 2): what quantity means on a buying document.<br>3. <b>The rates</b> — through the buying price lists and supplier-specific prices (Chapter 3), plus the system-maintained last purchase rate (Chapter 6).<br>4. <b>The planning fields</b> — lead time, safety stock, reorder levels (Chapter 5): the parameters module 3's demand machinery computes from.<br>5. <b>The valuation tie</b> — what the item truly costs is the valuation question the Inventory track's module 5 owns; this module supplies its inputs (Chapters 4, 7).</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The procurement function maintains the buying section; supplier rates change by the governed update, mirroring the selling side; planning fields change by decision with the replenishment review; and the standing rule: <b>a cost is a defined figure with a source — the quoted rate, the invoiced rate, and the landed valuation are three different numbers, named correctly on every document and decision.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: purchase units. Chapter 3: buying price lists. Chapter 4: the three cost figures. Chapter 5: the planning fields. Chapter 6: reading purchase prices. Chapter 7: landed-cost thinking. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Purchase units and the conversion arithmetic", "12", "<p>Suppliers sell in cartons, drums, and pallets; stock counts in units. This chapter runs the unit machinery inbound.</p><p><b>1. The Default Purchase UOM.</b> On the Item's purchasing section: the unit buying documents pre-fill with — set to how the item is normally bought (the carton for the standing pharmaceutical case), while stock continues to count in the stock unit. Any converted unit can be selected per document row; the default is the normal case, not a restriction.</p><p><b>2. The conversion table, inbound.</b> The same table the Selling track verified (Carton-12 = 12 units) serves buying, and the same rules apply with the inbound consequences stated:</p><p>1. <b>Factors come from the physical product, verified at the register.</b> On the inbound side, the supplier's carton contents are counted at first receipt and at any pack-size change — the supplier's catalogue is the claim; the count is the verification.<br>2. <b>A wrong factor mis-posts every receipt.</b> The receipt entered in cartons converts to stock units by the factor: a factor of 12 where the physical carton holds 10 overstates stock by a fifth on every receipt and understates unit cost by the same proportion — both errors silent until a count or a margin review surfaces them.<br>3. <b>The rate-and-unit pairing rule.</b> A rate belongs to a unit: ₦9,600 per carton and ₦800 per unit are the same price stated twice, and every document row is read as quantity-unit-rate together. The receiving discipline (module 6) names this pairing as its first check, because the carton-rate-entered-on-a-unit-line receipt is the classic inbound costing error — the Inventory track's remediation cases include exactly this pattern.</p><p><b>3. Pack-size changes.</b> A supplier changing carton contents is a governed, dated change: the factor updates, the carton-denominated rates update in the same decision (the pairing rule applied to maintenance), and open Purchase Orders in the old pack size are reviewed — an order for 100 old cartons received against new cartons mis-counts by the pack difference unless the order is amended first.</p><p><b>4. The purchase-unit read — quarterly.</b> Riding the item lifecycle reads: factors sampled against physical cartons for the top-purchased items; rate-unit pairings sampled on the quarter's receipts; items bought in units their table does not carry corrected by adding the decided unit. The read shares its calendar with the selling-side conversion read; one register, both directions confirmed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Buying price lists and supplier rates", "12", "<p>The buying side encodes who charges what, so documents fetch agreed rates instead of remembered ones. This chapter is the architecture.</p><p><b>1. The buying Price List.</b> Open Price List &gt; New with the Buying flag: the named context for purchase rates, in its currency. The standing architecture mirrors the selling side's restraint:</p><p>1. <b>The standard buying list</b> — the default naira context most domestic purchasing resolves to.<br>2. <b>Currency lists where suppliers bill in other currencies</b> — the US-dollar list the Indian manufacturer's rates live on, per module 1's currency structure.<br>3. <b>Additional lists only by decision</b> — the same anti-proliferation rule as selling: every list is a rate set to maintain.</p><p><b>2. Item Prices, buying.</b> One row per item per list (with UOM and validity where used): the agreed rate with the supplier — <b>the negotiated figure from module 4's quotation cycle or module 8's arrangements, entered by the governed update with its source recorded.</b> The rate on the list is a decision with a paper trail: the quotation, the arrangement, or the negotiation file it came from.</p><p><b>3. Supplier-specific rates.</b> Where the site maintains rates per supplier (the supplier's own quoted prices as distinct from a general buying list), the price machinery resolves the supplier's rate onto the supplier's documents. The maintenance rule is unchanged: rates enter from documents (the supplier's quotation or price circular, filed), by the governed update, and the price changes read reconciles the month's changes to their sources.</p><p><b>4. What the buying lists are for.</b> Their operational function, stated:</p><p>1. <b>Pre-fill</b> — the Purchase Order draws the agreed rate; the preparer verifies rather than recalls.<br>2. <b>Variance detection</b> — the invoice-versus-order rate check (module 7's matching) has a reference because the order had one.<br>3. <b>Negotiation memory</b> — the list states the current agreement; the update files state its history; the module 4 comparison worksheets state the alternatives considered. A price conversation with a supplier is conducted from these records.</p><p><b>5. The buying price reads — monthly.</b> The changes read: every buying Item Price change against its source document, per the fields-move-by-decision pattern. The completeness read: actively purchased items without a current rate on their supplier's context — the gap that forces typed rates on orders, which is the door this architecture closes. Both worked to zero with the update cycle.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The three figures called cost", "13", "<p>Three different numbers are all called cost. This chapter defines each, names its source, and states what each is for — because confusing them misprices decisions.</p><p><b>1. The quoted rate — what the supplier asks.</b> Source: the supplier's quotation, price circular, or arrangement (modules 4, 8), held on the buying lists. Definition: the agreed or offered price per unit in the supplier's currency, before anything happens. Used for: ordering decisions, comparisons between suppliers, and the Purchase Order's rate. Not used for: valuing stock or computing margin — the quoted rate omits everything between the supplier's gate and the business's shelf.</p><p><b>2. The invoiced rate — what the supplier charges.</b> Source: the Purchase Invoice (module 7). Definition: the price actually billed per unit, which the matching discipline compares to the order's quoted rate — a variance is a finding, not a correction to wave through. Used for: the payables liability, the matching control, and the price trend (Chapter 6). The invoiced rate is the commercial truth of one transaction; it is still not the shelf cost.</p><p><b>3. The landed valuation — what the shelf cost is.</b> Source: the stock ledger, per the Inventory track's module 5: the receipt's rate plus the capitalised acquisition costs — freight, clearing, duties, insurance — attached through the Landed Cost Voucher where they apply (Chapter 7). Definition: the per-unit value at which stock is carried and consumed — the figure Moving Average or FIFO maintains. Used for: margin arithmetic, pricing reviews (the selling side's price decisions read this figure), dead-stock and markdown decisions, and every valuation report. This is the only one of the three figures that may be called <b>cost</b> in a margin sentence.</p><p><b>4. The discipline, applied.</b></p><p>1. <b>On documents</b> — the Purchase Order carries the quoted rate; the invoice carries the invoiced rate; the stock ledger carries the valuation. No document's figure is typed over to match another; differences route through their controls (matching, Landed Cost Vouchers, the variance reads).<br>2. <b>In decisions</b> — a selling-price review that reads the quoted rate instead of the landed valuation understates cost by the acquisition layer and overstates margin by the same amount; on imported lines the gap is material (Chapter 7 quantifies it). The decision rule: <b>margin sentences use the landed valuation; negotiation sentences use the quoted and invoiced rates.</b><br>3. <b>In conversation</b> — the register's vocabulary names which figure is meant. The question <i>what does this item cost</i> receives a counter-question — for buying, for billing, or on the shelf — until the register's users ask it precisely themselves.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The planning fields", "12", "<p>The item master carries the parameters replenishment computes from. This chapter is each field, its honest value, and its review.</p><p><b>1. Lead Time Days.</b> Definition: the days from placing the order to stock being available for sale — order processing, the supplier's fulfilment, transit, receiving, and quality release, end to end. The honest value is measured, not hoped: the actual order-to-availability history per supplier (module 8's on-time data feeds it). Domestic manufacturers and the Indian import line carry very different figures, and each item's field carries its own supplier reality. Every reorder computation multiplies demand by this figure; an optimistic lead time is a stock-out generator by arithmetic.</p><p><b>2. Safety Stock.</b> Definition: the buffer held against demand and supply variability — the quantity intended to remain when the replenishment arrives on time. Set per the policy's method (days-of-sales for the standing case), higher where lead times are long or variable (the import line) and lower where replenishment is fast. Safety stock is a costed decision: it is money held on shelves deliberately, sized by the variability it protects against, and reviewed rather than ratcheted.</p><p><b>3. Reorder Level and Reorder Quantity.</b> The item-warehouse reorder table the Inventory track's module 6 operates: the level (demand over lead time plus safety stock) and the quantity per the ordering policy (the economic batch, the supplier's minimums, the carton multiples). This module's contribution is the inputs' honesty — the level computes from Chapter 5.1 and 5.2's figures, and the quantity respects the purchase unit's realities (orders in carton multiples per Chapter 2).</p><p><b>4. Minimum Order Quantity and the supplier constraints.</b> The item's MOQ field where suppliers impose one, and the practical constraints the ordering policy records: the supplier's carton or pallet multiples, the price-break thresholds (the module 4 quotations state them), and the import line's container arithmetic (Chapter 7). Replenishment quantities are shaped by these constraints deliberately — the reorder review documents where a constraint forces ordering above the computed need, because that excess is a costed consequence of the supply structure, visible rather than accidental.</p><p><b>5. The planning review — with the reorder review's cadence.</b> The fields against reality: lead times re-measured from the period's receipts (the module 8 on-time read supplies the actuals); safety stock against the period's stock-outs and excesses; reorder parameters against what the period actually demanded. Fields move by decision at the review, dated — a planning field changed mid-period by an unrecorded edit is the configuration drift the change read catches.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Reading purchase prices: the last purchase rate and the trend", "12", "<p>The system records what the business actually paid. This chapter is the reading of that record — and the boundaries of its use.</p><p><b>1. Last Purchase Rate.</b> Maintained automatically: the rate on the item's most recent purchase, per unit. Its uses:</p><p>1. <b>The reasonableness check</b> — the order preparer sees the new rate against the last: a movement is verified against its cause (a known price change, a currency movement, a pack-size change) before the order proceeds.<br>2. <b>The quick answer</b> — what the business last paid, without opening the ledger.</p><p><b>2. What the last purchase rate is not.</b> The boundaries, stated:</p><p>1. <b>Not the valuation</b> — stock on the shelf carries the method's figure (Moving Average across receipts, or FIFO's layers), not the latest rate; the two diverge whenever prices move, and margin arithmetic uses the valuation (Chapter 4's rule).<br>2. <b>Not the agreed rate</b> — the buying list states the current agreement; the last rate states the last transaction. Where they differ, the difference is a question: an unapplied price change, a one-off premium, or an error the matching read should have caught.<br>3. <b>Not a pricing authority</b> — a Purchase Order rate is justified by its list or its quotation, not by repetition of the last rate; repetition compounds any error in it.</p><p><b>3. The price trend read — monthly.</b> Purchase rates per item across periods, for the top items by spend:</p><p>1. <b>The movement list</b> — items whose rate moved beyond the threshold, each with its explanation written (the supplier's increase and its notice, the currency movement on import lines, a pack-size change misread as a price change — the pairing rule's audit).<br>2. <b>The margin hand-off</b> — confirmed cost increases route to the selling side's pricing review (the module 2 governed update on the selling lists), because a cost increase absorbed silently is a margin decrease decided by nobody.<br>3. <b>The negotiation file</b> — the trend per supplier accumulates as the material of the module 8 review conversations: what moved, when, with what notice, against what market reference where one exists.</p><p><b>4. The variance connection.</b> The trend read works alongside module 7's matching: matching catches the single invoice that disagrees with its order; the trend catches the drift that arrives in small agreed steps. Both file monthly, and together they are the buying side's price evidence — the record every negotiation and every review draws on.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Landed-cost thinking", "13", "<p>For imported goods, the factory price is the beginning of the cost, not the cost. This chapter is the landed computation and its operational rules.</p><p><b>1. The landed cost, defined.</b> The per-unit value of imported stock on the shelf: the supplier's invoice value plus every cost incurred to bring the goods to usable condition and location — international freight, marine insurance, customs duties and levies, clearing agent charges, port and terminal charges, local haulage — capitalised into the stock's valuation rather than expensed. The Inventory track's module 5 covered the mechanism (the Landed Cost Voucher attaching costs to a receipt and re-rating its stock); this chapter is the buying-side computation and habits.</p><p><b>2. The landed worksheet.</b> Per import consignment, the standing worksheet computes the expected landed cost before commitment:</p><p>1. <b>The goods value</b> — the supplier's price in its currency, converted at the planning rate.<br>2. <b>The acquisition layer</b> — freight per the forwarder's quote; insurance per the policy's rate; duties per the tariff classification the clearing agent confirms (the classification is confirmed in advance, because a tariff surprise at the port is a landed-cost surprise after commitment); clearing, port, and haulage per the standing rates.<br>3. <b>The per-unit result</b> — the consignment's total landed cost divided over its units: the figure the ordering decision, and later the selling-price review, actually needs.</p><p><b>3. The comparison rule.</b> Suppliers are compared landed, never ex-works: the domestic manufacturer's naira rate and the Indian manufacturer's dollar rate are incomparable until both are stated as naira-per-unit-on-the-shelf. The module 4 quotation comparisons apply this rule as procedure — the comparison worksheet carries a landed column, and the decision minutes record landed figures.</p><p><b>4. Consignment arithmetic.</b> The import line's quantities are shaped by the consignment: freight and clearing are substantially fixed per shipment, so the per-unit acquisition layer falls as the consignment fills — the container arithmetic that makes import ordering a batch decision rather than a reorder-point decision alone. The planning consequence (Chapter 5): import items order in consignment-sized batches on the import calendar, with safety stock covering the longer, lumpier cycle.</p><p><b>5. After arrival: the reconciliation.</b> The worksheet's expected figures reconcile to actuals when the consignment lands: the Landed Cost Voucher applies the actual charges to the receipt (the Inventory mechanism), and the worksheet-versus-actual comparison files with the consignment — the recurring differences (a forwarder consistently above quote, a duty line misclassified) are the findings the next consignment's worksheet corrects. The read keeps the planning figures honest, and it is the difference between an import operation that knows its costs and one that discovers them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: PARA-500's buying architecture", "13", "<p>This chapter completes the record whose selling side the Selling track built. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the one record confirmed (Chapter 1).</b> PARA-500 — Paracetamol 500mg Tablets — already exists in the register with its selling section complete. The buying section completes on the same record: Is Purchase Item on; the buying defaults per the accountant's design. The rejected alternative is recorded in the register note: no PARA-500-BUY record — a second record would split stock, cost history, and planning into two half-records.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the purchase units (Chapter 2).</b> Default Purchase UOM: Carton-12 — Emzor supplies in cartons of twelve. The factor was verified physically at first receipt (the Inventory track's register discipline); the pairing rule files with the register: the carton rate <b>₦9,600</b> and the unit rate ₦800 are one price, and every order and receipt row is read quantity-unit-rate together.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the buying rates (Chapter 3).</b> The standard buying list carries PARA-500 at ₦9,600 per Carton-12, entered by the governed update with its source: the Emzor arrangement from the module 4 cycle. The completeness read confirms every actively purchased item on the range carries its current rate; the changes read's first month reconciles to sources exactly.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the three costs stated (Chapter 4).</b> For PARA-500 the three figures are written into the register note as the vocabulary example: the quoted rate ₦9,600 per carton (the Emzor agreement); the invoiced rate as billed and matched per delivery; the landed valuation — for this domestic line, effectively the receipt rate, since the acquisition layer is the supplier's included delivery. The contrast case is the Indian manufacturer's generic line, where the worksheet shows the dollar goods value carrying a material acquisition layer — freight, duty, clearing — before it becomes a shelf figure, and where a selling-price review reading the quoted dollar rate instead of the landed naira figure would overstate margin on every unit.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the planning fields (Chapter 5).</b> PARA-500's fields enter from measured reality: the domestic lead time from Emzor's actual order-to-availability history; safety stock per the days-of-sales policy at the item's velocity; the reorder row per warehouse with the quantity in carton multiples. The import generics carry their own figures: the long lead time measured across the consignment cycle, the larger safety stock the variability requires, and consignment-batch ordering per Chapter 7's arithmetic.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the first trend read (Chapter 6).</b> Month one's read lists two movements: a notified Emzor increase on one line (explanation written; routed to the selling pricing review — the governed update runs on the selling list the same week), and an apparent jump on an import line that the pairing audit resolves as a pack-size change, not a price change — the factor and rates corrected together per Chapter 2's rule. Both findings file; neither is absorbed silently.</p><p><b>The architecture evidence.</b> One record with both sides complete; verified units with the pairing rule; rates with sources; the three costs named; planning fields from measured figures; the trend read operating. Module 3 builds the demand machinery on these foundations.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in purchase items and costs, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the second buying record.</b> Symptom: a PARA-500-BUY beside PARA-500; stock and cost history split; planning computing from half the picture. Correction: the merge remediation per the lifecycle rules. Prevention: the one-record principle — the buying section completes on the existing record.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the carton rate on a unit line.</b> Symptom: a receipt or order row whose rate belongs to a different unit than its quantity; unit costs wrong by the conversion factor; margins misstated until a review surfaces it. Correction: the document corrected through its lane; the valuation repaired per the Inventory procedures where it posted. Prevention: the pairing rule — quantity, unit, rate read together on every row — and the receiving discipline's first check.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the typed order rate.</b> Symptom: Purchase Order rates recalled rather than fetched; agreed rates unapplied; matching without a reference. Correction: the buying lists completed; open orders verified. Prevention: rates on lists from sources, and the completeness read.</p><p><b>Error 4 — margin from the wrong cost.</b> Symptom: selling-price reviews reading quoted rates; import margins overstated by the acquisition layer; loss-making lines reading as profitable. Correction: the reviews re-run on landed valuations. Prevention: the vocabulary rule — margin sentences use the landed valuation.</p><p><b>Error 5 — optimistic planning fields.</b> Symptom: lead times entered as hopes; stock-outs by arithmetic; safety stock ratcheted after each incident instead of sized by review. Correction: fields re-entered from measured history. Prevention: the planning review on the reorder cadence, with module 8's actuals feeding it.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the silent cost drift.</b> Symptom: small agreed increases accumulating unread; selling prices static while costs climb; the margin decrease nobody decided. Correction: the trend read run and the pricing hand-off executed. Prevention: the monthly trend read with written explanations and the routing rule to the selling review.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the buying section on the one record; operate purchase units with verified factors and the pairing rule; maintain buying lists with sourced rates and the two monthly reads; define the three cost figures with their sources and uses; complete the planning fields from measured reality; read the last purchase rate within its boundaries and run the trend read; and compute a landed worksheet with the comparison rule and consignment arithmetic.</p><p><b>Module rule 2: a cost is a defined figure with a source — one item record serving both sides, units paired with their rates, agreed rates held on lists from documents, the quoted, invoiced, and landed figures named correctly in every decision, planning fields measured not hoped, and every price movement read and routed.</b> Module 3 covers Material Requests and the demand machinery.</p>"),
]

